2026-09-20 identity design convergence: a sealed-memfd, inode-scoped marker is viable for one KVM execution without a socket queue ledger, subject to a runtime capability gate. Use MFD_ALLOW_SEALING; immutable path/authority data in the reserved memfd name; exact F_SEAL_WRITE|F_SEAL_GROW|F_SEAL_SHRINK|F_SEAL_SEAL; a fresh non-guest-visible 256-bit process-tree HMAC key; and the complete opaque shmem name_to_handle_at handle/type plus mount/device scope, path, size, content and seals as a length-prefixed MAC transcript. Store a uniformly rejection-sampled approximately 92.897-bit tag only in raw mtime (nonnegative time64 seconds below TIME64_MAX plus nanoseconds below 1e9), then require exact timestamp readback. Do not use atime: reads can update it. Linux 5.8 source has shmem export handles when CONFIG_TMPFS is enabled, but version alone is not enough; runtime qualification must prove handle and nanosecond-mtime stability across duplicate, fresh proc-fd reopen, O_PATH and SCM_RIGHTS. The inode marker naturally survives last-sender-close, MSG_PEEK, MSG_CTRUNC, dup, reopen, O_PATH, fork and exec without changing ancillary payloads or maintaining queue state. Reserved-name candidates with a bad version, foreign authority, bad handle/seals/content/mtime, or an unavailable capability must fail closed and transactionally roll back receive. This is an execution-local provenance mechanism, not a root/kernel security boundary: external native peers can alter mtime or mode and cause deterministic rejection, but cannot forge a different sealed inode except by roughly a 2^-92.9 online guess; cross-supervisor acceptance is unsupported unless authority is shared. Direct synthetic-proc fchmod must become EPERM after O_PATH precedence, and fdinfo remains nontransferable. Before syncfs lands, tests must cover every transcript-field mutation, distinct-object replay, last-close/peek/truncation/multi-right rollback, proc-fd reopen and O_PATH, fork/exec/CLOEXEC, capability failure, and exact ordinary-memfd error forwarding.
